                    coach_r1_speed:         `<strong>Treating MRD 6 bn as the prize is the headline error.</strong> Open everything at once and you capture the gains on paper — then the competitiveness index falls below the PM’s 60 floor, garments shed 88,000 jobs, and the schedule is politically dead by month 14. Integration is a sequencing problem, not a speed contest.`,
                    coach_r1_reject:        `<strong>Dismissing the baseline outright is reflexive protectionism.</strong> Protect every job and phase everything to 36 months and you deliver almost no gains while a rival capital writes the rules of origin. The baseline isn’t the enemy — it’s the welfare yardstick you sequence <em>against</em>.`,
                    coach_r1_norigour:      `<strong>Thin analysis breeds bad schedules.</strong> Without scoring RCA and adjustment costs sector by sector, you’re guessing which sectors are offensive and which are defensive — and a misclassified schedule is exactly what breaches the floor later.`,
                    coach_r2_immediate:     `<strong>Immediate elimination books the big trade number — and the headline failure.</strong> Garments shed displacement at the top of the range, the competitiveness index plunges toward the 60 floor, and the full MRD 2.1 bn tariff hole opens at once. The gain is real on a spreadsheet; the schedule that produced it won’t survive the politics.`,
                    coach_r2_phased:        `<strong>That is the modelled sequence.</strong> Offensive sectors open fast to capture gains; defensive sectors phase over 36 months so adjustment support has time to land. You keep the index up, cushion the worst of the displacement, and only open the tariff hole gradually. Sequencing, not speed.`,
                    coach_r2_slow:          `<strong>Phasing everything to 36 months is reflexive protectionism.</strong> You protect jobs and deliver almost no gains — and you’ve now tabled a schedule slower than the bloc minimum. The Secretariat notes it, a rival capital writes the rules of origin, and you’ve forfeited MRD 3.4 bn and your seat.`,
                    coach_r2_fulllabour:    `<strong>Full recognition with no retention is one-sided labour math.</strong> Services exports jump — but 13,400 nurses and engineers leave in year one, three regional hospitals are understaffed, and your transformational sectors lose the very talent you were investing to upgrade. Did you model the outflow, or only the inflow?`,
                    coach_r2_meet:          `<strong>Meeting the bloc minimum keeps you at the table.</strong> Co-financing secured, rule-setting seat held — and with a sequenced schedule you’re shaping the single market rather than reacting to it. That is the swing-vote position used well.`,
                    coach_r2_below:         `<strong>Tabling below the minimum cedes the agenda.</strong> You’ve forfeited MRD 3.4 bn and your seat on the rule-setting committee — so who is writing the rules of origin now, you or a rival capital? Defensiveness has a strategic cost beyond foregone gains.`,
                    coach_r2_overshoot:     `<strong>Opening faster than the bloc even asks is speed for its own sake.</strong> You look like the model member for one quarter — then the competitiveness floor and the garment workforce pay for the gesture. There is no prize for over-liberalizing past your adjustment capacity.`,
                    coach_r3_evensplit:     `<strong>Six tidy slices feel fair and fund nothing.</strong> Wage insurance at that level covers only 40% of displaced garment workers for four months, the rural cross-subsidy gap is still MRD 1.4 bn, and no claim clears its adjustment threshold. Scarce funds demand prioritization, not equality.`,
                    coach_r3_noretrain:     `<strong>Under-funding retraining leaves displaced workers exposed.</strong> Wage insurance and retraining are what make the politically impossible feasible — starve that line and the displacement converts straight into a competitiveness and political hit.`,
                    coach_r3_nofiscal:      `<strong>You’ve left the tariff hole open.</strong> Eliminating the sensitive-list tariffs erases MRD 2.1 bn a year that cross-subsidizes rural development. Fund no replacement and the Finance Minister sees a 4.4% structural gap — integration that breaks the fisc is a deferred crisis.`,
                    coach_r3_notalent:      `<strong>You unleashed the brain drain and didn’t fund retention.</strong> Bonded scholarships, return incentives and wage convergence are the counter-levers — without them the nurses and engineers you needed for the upgrade keep leaving, and the competitiveness agenda bleeds.`,
                    coach_r3_talentsave:    `<strong>Funding retention stanched the outflow.</strong> Return incentives and wage convergence pulled the brain-drain number back down and kept talent in the transformational sectors — the upside of recognition without the full cost.`,
                    coach_r3_prioritized:   `<strong>That is prioritization, not equality.</strong> You funded retraining and the rural cross-subsidy past their thresholds and held a sensible reserve. Some claims went short — but the ones that keep the floor and the fisc intact cleared the bar. That is what a scarce fund demands.`,
                    coach_r4_safeguard:     `<strong>A permitted safeguard plus accelerated retraining is the right recovery.</strong> It is legal under the protocol, targeted at the dumped-garment shock, and it lifts the index back above 60 — without suspending the schedule or forfeiting the co-financing. You bought time without ceding the agenda.`,
                    coach_r4_forfeit:       `<strong>Suspending the whole schedule over-corrects.</strong> You stopped the bleeding, but pausing all liberalization forfeits the MRD 3.4 bn co-financing and your seat — and tells the bloc Maritima can’t hold a schedule under pressure. A targeted safeguard would have held the floor without the surrender.`,
                    coach_r4_nothing:       `<strong>Holding the line through a dumping shock is how the floor breaks.</strong> The index was already at 60.4; do nothing and the next quarter takes it under 60, the schedule is politically dead, and you forfeit the co-financing anyway. The safeguard exists for exactly this moment.`,
                    coach_r4_rebtrade:      `<strong>Pouring the marginal peso into trade-facilitation polishes the gains story while the floor wobbles.</strong> The shock is hitting garment workers, not the ports — the money the moment needs is wage-insurance and retraining, not another customs window.`,
                    coach_r4_rebnone:       `<strong>Leaving the allocation untouched in a shock assumes the plan was shock-proof.</strong> It wasn’t — that’s why the index is at 60.4. A contingency you never deploy is just an under-spend; move it to where the shock landed.`,
                    coach_r4_balanced:      `<strong>That is the summit pitch a Secretariat trusts.</strong> Gains captured, floor held, jobs cushioned, fisc covered — and the sequencing logic that connects them. You didn’t chase one number; you defended a coherent schedule on all four KPIs.`,
                    coach_r4_pitchgains:    `<strong>Leading with the trade number alone invites the obvious question.</strong> “What was the gain worth if the program collapses?” A Secretariat can see all four KPIs. Wave the biggest figure and downplay the rest, and they’ll assume the rest is worse than it is.`,
                    coach_r4_pitchprotect:  `<strong>Defending how few jobs you lost concedes the strategic ground.</strong> Protection is only half the scorecard — the bloc will ask what the foregone gains and the surrendered seat cost. A schedule defended on jobs alone reads as a retreat dressed as prudence.`,

                    sum_default:            `Decision tabled.`,
                    sum_r1_good:            `Electronics offensive, garments defensive, baseline read as a welfare yardstick.`,
